
// The slow.loc example was built and run with the compiler of 2026-10-13.

[[caching]]
=== Caching

Mark a label `cache: true` in the program YAML and every call through it is
memoized to disk. The next call with equal arguments, against unchanged
source code, is served from the cache rather than computed again, in this run
or any later one.

The freshness check is content-based, not time-based. Morloc has
*no* notion of "the source file is newer than the cache entry, so
re-run." Editing a comment in an unrelated function will not
invalidate any cache. Copying the program to a new path will not
either. Two builds on two machines that emit byte-identical pool
sources share the same cache namespace. This is a deliberate
departure from `make`, `snakemake`, `nextflow`, and similar tools
that use mtime as a freshness signal and routinely re-run the
world after a `git checkout` or a clock skew.

The build parameters passed with `-X` (see the build parameters
section) also participate in the key, since they can change the
compiled output without changing the pool source -- switching a
Futhark backend or adding a compiler flag is treated as a distinct
build rather than a cache hit.

==== Declaring a cached call

[source, morloc]
.slow.loc
----
module slow (square)

import root-py
source Py from "slow.py" ("slow_square")

slow_square :: Int -> Int

square :: Int -> Int
square x = expensive@slow_square x
----

[source, python]
.slow.py
----
import sys

def slow_square(x):
    print("computing", x, file=sys.stderr)
    return x * x
----

[source, yaml]
.slow.yaml
----
labeled-groups:
  expensive: { cache: true }
----

The first call computes, and the second finds the result on disk:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o slow slow.loc
$ ./slow square 4
computing 4
16
$ ./slow square 4
16
$ ./slow square 5
computing 5
25
----

The same group config also controls per-step logging (`log: true`); the two
flags are independent and may be combined.

==== What a cached label covers

A label wraps one call of the function it is written on, and the cache
answers for that call alone. Two things follow:

 - The call's arguments are computed before the cache is checked, so they are
   computed even on a hit. In `expensive@slow_square (prepare x)`, `prepare x`
   runs every time. To skip it on a hit, name the whole computation and label
   the name: with `squarePrepared x = slow_square (prepare x)`, write
   `expensive@squarePrepared x`.
 - A label on a suspension (see <<effects>>), or on a call that returns one,
   caches each run of the suspension: the first run computes and stores its
   result, and every later run with the same captured values reads it.

`cache: true` is your claim that the call returns the same value whenever it
is made with the same arguments. Morloc does not check it, so do not cache a
call whose result should change from one run to the next.

A call that fails is not cached. Running it again with the same arguments
fails the same way rather than returning a value that was never computed.

[[cache-layout]]
==== Where the cache lives

The cache lives under the first of these that is set:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Source | Notes

| `MORLOC_CACHE_BASE` env var | Explicit override. Useful for Docker
bind mounts and shared filesystems where SLURM workers need access
to the same cache.
| `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/morloc/cache` | If `XDG_CACHE_HOME` is set.
| `~/.cache/morloc/cache` | Default.
|===

Inside, each label has a directory of small index files, and the values
themselves are stored once each under `data/`, shared by every label:

----
~/.cache/morloc/cache/
|-- expensive/
|   |-- 98c21d2ac043688f.dat
|   `-- bf38289c8a399429.dat
`-- data/
    |-- 7c2e0dc2ec1a8103.dat
    `-- ef97ee16737dff50.dat
----

Morloc never deletes an entry that is still valid. Deleting the whole
directory, or one label's directory, is safe while no program is using it,
and empties the cache. How a call's key is computed and how entries are
written safely is specified in <<eco-caching>>.

